-0.0205841064453125, 0.035797119140625, 0.0248870849609375, 0.024169921875, -0.031341552734375, 0.015655517578125, 0.00933837890625, 0.0199737548828125, -0.04913330078125, -0.0010824203491210938, -0.00273895263671875, -0.00905609130859375, 0.0296783447265625, 0.00867462158203125, -0.0214996337890625, 0.026123046875, 0.0126953125, 0.0131072998046875, -0.00783538818359375, 0.03271484375, 0.0030422210693359375, -0.0211181640625, 0.007732391357421875, 0.0176544189453125, 0.01885986328125, -0.01099395751953125, 0.040679931640625, -0.009857177734375, 0.0231781005859375, -0.0191192626953125, -0.0217437744140625, 0.0210418701171875, -0.0258331298828125, -0.0169677734375, -0.0168914794921875, 0.01751708984375, 0.030548095703125, 0.0230255126953125, -0.03240966796875, -0.01436614990234375, 0.0228424072265625, 0.0196685791015625, 0.0169677734375, 0.00274658203125, 0.009796142578125, 0.0635986328125, 0.01401519775390625, -0.0261993408203125, -0.021209716796875, 0.0216217041015625, 0.01666259765625, 0.007965087890625, 0.01439666748046875, 0.0228729248046875, -0.01947021484375, 0.019622802734375, 0.0038967132568359375, -0.02838134765625, -0.004180908203125, 0.030303955078125, 0.015899658203125, -0.0304107666015625, 0.02581787109375, 0.028839111328125, -0.0101776123046875, 0.005481719970703125, 0.0233154296875, -0.0200958251953125, 0.03179931640625, -0.005420684814453125, 0.0019855499267578125, 0.03759765625, 0.002819061279296875, -0.017913818359375, 0.02288818359375, -0.034881591796875, -0.00464630126953125, -0.01287841796875, -0.0024471282958984375, 0.00534820556640625, 0.05963134765625, -0.010833740234375, -0.00919342041015625, 0.021759033203125, -0.04693603515625, 0.028656005859375, 0.0289764404296875, -0.01678466796875, 0.0016717910766601562, 0.0014047622680664062, -0.0086212158203125, 0.00905609130859375, 0.010101318359375, 0.046966552734375, -0.038726806640625, -0.0036468505859375, 0.0240936279296875, 0.043212890625, 0.042327880859375, 0.012969970703125, 0.01108551025390625, -0.03466796875, -0.023162841796875, 0.00917816162109375, -0.01904296875, -0.01274871826171875, 0.046722412109375, 0.038238525390625, 0.035400390625, 0.0253753662109375, 0.00801849365234375, 0.0294342041015625, 0.0113372802734375, -0.034912109375, -0.0025959014892578125, 0.005794525146484375, 0.01462554931640625, -0.0200042724609375, -0.0031890869140625, 0.02099609375, -0.025726318359375, -0.037322998046875, 0.017608642578125, -0.007568359375, -0.005580902099609375, -0.004955291748046875, -0.0277099609375, -0.01146697998046875, 0.0042266845703125, -0.0195159912109375, -0.0308380126953125, 0.0180511474609375, -0.021392822265625, 0.0247344970703125, 0.01261138916015625, -0.030731201171875, -0.0100555419921875, 0.0090179443359375, 0.00884246826171875, -0.020050048828125, 0.015960693359375, 0.0160064697265625, -0.00716400146484375, 0.01153564453125, -0.0201416015625, -0.01009368896484375, 0.0238494873046875, -0.017181396484375, 0.0048675537109375, 0.011749267578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the use of Geometric Algebra (GA) in the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems\u2014most notably in the study of electrical circuits (see, e.g., [1], [2], [3]). Geometric Algebra extends traditional algebraic structures by providing multivectors\u2014objects comprising scalars, vectors, bivectors, and higher-grade elements, each distinguished by their grade (i.e., the number of basis vectors in their exterior product). The algebra is further characterized by its signature, which specifies the numbers of basis elements with positive and negative square norms; for instance, signature (3,0) corresponds to a standard three-dimensional Euclidean space. In systems analysis and design, transforming the ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that describe system behavior from the time domain to the Laplace domain is foundational, enabling analysis of stability, frequency response, and solution structure.\n\n**Motivation and Need for Generalization:**  \nTraditional Laplace transforms are well established for real- and complex-valued functions, and have been extended to select hypercomplex algebras such as quaternions and Clifford algebras ([4], [5]). However, these extensions are typically limited: for example, [4] addresses quaternionic Laplace transforms primarily restricted to scalar-valued or specific quaternion-valued signals with commutative multiplication. Similarly, [5] describes Laplace-like transforms on hyperbolic (split-complex) numbers. These cases do not accommodate the full generality of geometric algebras, which can encode physical quantities as mixed-grade multivectors (combining, for example, vector and bivector components) undergoing noncommutative multiplication. Current definitions thus fail when a system\u2019s state, input, or output are modeled as arbitrary multivector functions; for example, in 3D circuit analysis, the current may be represented as a combination of vectors (for current flow), bivectors (for magnetic flux), and scalars in a single time-dependent multivector I(t). If only scalar or low-grade components are transformable, analyses leveraging the holistic structure of GA are incomplete or unavailable.\n\n**Precise Problem and Main Objectives:**  \nThe specific problem addressed in this work is the absence of a rigorous and comprehensive framework for the Laplace transform of *arbitrary* multivector-valued functions\u2014functions \\( F : \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) denotes a geometric algebra of signature (p,q). Prior approaches typically:  \n- Treat only scalar or simple vector components, neglecting the general case of mixed-grade multivectors.  \n- Assume commutative multiplication for simplicity, while most geometric algebras have inherently noncommutative products (e.g., vector\u2013bivector multiplication).  \n- Restrict to low-dimensional algebras or subclasses, limiting their use in physical applications involving complex multivector interactions.\n\nIn light of these shortcomings, our objectives are:  \n1. To provide a constructive and generalizable Laplace transform definition applicable to arbitrary multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras.  \n2. To prove foundational properties\u2014including linearity, application to derivatives, and invertibility\u2014while respecting the graded, generally noncommutative algebraic structure.  \n3. To clarify what is thereby enabled, such as direct Laplace analysis of physical systems whose states, currents, or fields are genuine multivector-valued functions.\n\n**Terminology and Formal Overview:**  \nLet \\( M(t) = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\langle M(t) \\rangle_r \\) be a general multivector-valued function of time, where \\( \\langle \\cdot \\rangle_r \\) denotes projection onto grade-r components (with n the total number of basis vectors for the algebra\u2019s signature). Formally, our Laplace transform for multivectors is defined componentwise:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{M\\}(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} M(t) dt = \\sum_{r=0}^n \\mathcal{L}\\{\\langle M(t) \\rangle_r\\}(s)\n\\]\nwith the integral taken in the geometric algebra, and multiplication/interactions between e.g., \\( s \\) and multivectors defined via the algebra\u2019s product. Key challenges addressed include:\n- Properly defining the exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) when s is a scalar but M(t) may have multigrade structure.\n- Ensuring that each grade transforms linearly and that properties such as the derivative rule (\\( \\mathcal{L}\\{M'(t)\\} = s\\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\} - M(0) \\)) generalize, possibly requiring careful attention to noncommuting elements.\n- Invertibility: showing that, under mild regularity conditions, the Laplace transform uniquely maps time-domain multivector functions to frequency domain and back.\n\n**Representative Main Result:**  \n*Theorem (Linearity and Differentiation):*  \nLet \\( M(t) \\) and \\( N(t) \\) be \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\)-valued functions that are absolutely integrable on \\( [0, \\infty) \\), and let \\( a, b \\in \\mathbb{R} \\). Then,\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{a M(t) + b N(t)\\}(s) = a \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) + b \\mathcal{L}\\{N(t)\\}(s)\n\\]\nand  \n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\frac{dM}{dt}(t)\\right\\}(s) = s \\mathcal{L}\\{M(t)\\}(s) - M(0)\n\\]\nwhere identities hold componentwise for all grades, and the product with \\( s \\) respects the grading and possible noncommutativity.\n\n*Explicit Example:*  \nConsider an RLC circuit element with current \\( I(t) = I_0(t)e_1 + \\Phi(t) e_{23} \\), where \\( e_1 \\) is a vector and \\( e_{23} \\) a bivector (magnetic flux). The proposed framework computes \\( \\mathcal{L}\\{I(t)\\}(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{I_0(t)\\}(s) e_1 + \\mathcal{L}\\{\\Phi(t)\\}(s) e_{23} \\), allowing full Laplace-domain analysis of mixed-grade physical quantities.\n\n**Applicability and Immediate Consequences:**  \nOur results enable rigorous Laplace-domain analysis of continuous-time systems in applications such as (but not limited to) RLC electrical circuits modeled in three-dimensional GA (signature (3,0)), spacetime electrodynamics (signature (1,3)), and robotics kinematics. This facilitates, for the first time, unified treatment of mixed-grade, noncommutative multivector signals\u2014extending what is possible in standard or previously extended Laplace analysis frameworks.\n\n**Main Contributions:**  \n- A formal, constructive definition of the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras of signature \u22645.\n- Proofs that core properties (linearity, differentiability, and invertibility) hold in this setting, with explicit respect for grading and noncommutativity.\n- Demonstrated application to multivector-valued systems in physics and engineering, including illustrative examples as above.\n- *Clarified comparison:* Unlike [4], which handles only restricted quaternionic-valued transforms, and [5], which is limited to hyperbolic numbers and commutative cases, our formulation applies directly and fully to arbitrary multivectors in general GA, encompassing mixed grades and noncommutative multiplication.\n\n**Limitations and Future Scope:**  \nThe present investigation restricts attention to algebras with signature \u22645 primarily due to an exponential rise in basis elements, computational complexity, and subtler algebraic identities as dimension increases (see [1], [3] for discussion). Beyond dimensionality, other limitations include (i) increased numerical complexity for high-grade elements that may hinder practical implementation; (ii) ambiguity in physical interpretation when modeling quantities not directly associated with measurable observables; and (iii) possible challenges in integrating fully general GA Laplace transforms with standard engineering analysis pipelines, which often expect scalar or vector signals. Addressing these issues and extending the framework to higher signature, high-performance computation, and workflow integration remain open directions.\n\n[1] Doran, C., & Lasenby, A. (2003). Geometric Algebra for Physicists. Cambridge University Press.  \n[2] Hitzer, E., & Sangwine, S. J. (Eds.). (2013). Quaternion and Clifford Fourier Transforms and Wavelets. Springer.  \n[3] Lasenby, J., Lasenby, A., & Doran, C. (2000). A Unified Mathematical Language for Physics and Engineering in the 21st Century. Phil. Trans. R. Soc. Lond. A 358, 21-39.  \n[4] Kou, K. I., & Liu, L. (2007). Quaternionic Laplace transforms and their applications. Mathematical Methods in the Applied Sciences, 30(15), 1785\u20131797.  \n[5] Sobczyk, G. (1995). The Hyperbolic Number Plane. The College Mathematics Journal, 26(4), 268\u2013280."}}